\section{Tab modules}{

Each tab is a Shiny module instance. \code{ui(id, type)} is the module's UI
function: it receives the tab's namespaced id and returns the tab's
content, so inputs and outputs inside it are created with
\verb{ns = shiny::NS(id); ns("name")}, or by passing \code{id} (or \code{ns("inner")})
to another module's UI function. \code{server(input, output, session, type)} is
the module's server function, run with \code{\link[shiny:moduleServer]{shiny::moduleServer()}} for the
same id, so \code{input$name} and \code{output$name} refer to that tab's elements and
nested modules are started as usual (\code{inner_server("inner")}).

Whatever \code{server()} returns is kept as the tab's \emph{handle}: it is passed to
\code{can_close()} and \code{on_close()} and available through the returned
\code{handle(id)}, so tabs can expose reactives and controls to the app.

Panes are kept in the page while hidden (invisible, not removed from
layout), so their contents keep their size and state while another tab is
selected.
}

\section{Closing}{

When a tab is closed by the user, \code{can_close(handle, tab)} decides what
happens: \code{TRUE} closes it, \code{FALSE} keeps it, and a character string asks
the user to confirm in a modal dialog showing that string. \code{on_close()} is
called just before a tab is removed, however it was closed, for cleanup
such as stopping a process. \code{tab} is a list with \code{id}, \code{type} and \code{title}.
}

